# Added after the previous card deck so saved learner ratings keep their meaning.
CLASSROOM_FOCUS = {
 'Numbers, surds & intervals': ('Number sets and safe notation', 'Natural numbers, whole numbers and integers form nested subsets of the rationals; every real number is rational or irrational. A terminating or recurring decimal is rational. Division by zero is undefined: it is not a complex number. Rounding changes a representation’s precision, so keep unrounded values until a final answer is required.'),
 'Algebra & equations': ('Products, factors and fractions', 'Treat factorisation as the reverse of multiplication. Take out a common factor first, then check the product by expanding. Algebraic fraction restrictions come from the original denominator and survive cancellation. When adding fractions, use a common denominator and combine whole numerators before simplifying.'),
 'Exponents & number patterns': ('Find a rule, then check it', 'For an arithmetic pattern, compare consecutive differences, express the common difference in Tₙ = a + (n − 1)d, and verify more than one supplied term. A solved position n must be a positive integer. For powers, apply exponent laws to common bases; powers of sums require expansion rather than distributing the exponent over addition.'),
 'Functions & transformations': ('Read a graph as a set of relationships', 'Identify intercepts, turning points, asymptotes and intervals of increasing or decreasing behaviour. To solve f(x) > g(x), first locate intersections, then compare which graph is above the other on each relevant interval. Check endpoints and excluded values instead of giving only a sketch.'),
 'Statistics & data handling': ('Describe centre and spread together', 'A mean, median or mode describes an aspect of the centre; range, quartiles and the interquartile range describe spread. Read the scale and units before comparing displays. An outlier can change the mean and range strongly, while the median and interquartile range may be less affected. A summary statistic alone cannot describe the entire distribution.'),
 'Financial maths': ('Use a timeline when the balance changes', 'Put deposits, withdrawals and rate changes on a timeline. Grow the current balance to each event, make the stated change, and then apply later interest to the new balance. Match the interest rate’s period with the number of periods and round the final amount as requested.'),
 'Euclidean geometry & measurement': ('Reasons and dimensions', 'In a geometry proof, pair each angle or side claim with a valid reason and use only established facts. Similarity fixes shape but permits a scale change. When calculating measurement, keep linear units, square units and cubic units distinct; an enlargement multiplies area by k² and volume by k³.'),
 'Probability & Venn diagrams': ('Make the regions account for every outcome', 'Place the intersection in a two-set Venn diagram before the exclusive regions. Include the outside region when the sample space contains neither-event outcomes. Check that all disjoint regions add to the sample total. A complement concerns all outcomes not in an event, while mutual exclusivity concerns an empty intersection.'),
 'Complex numbers & the Argand plane': ('Move between algebra and the diagram', 'Represent a + bi by (a, b), use the modulus as its distance from the origin, and interpret conjugation as reflection in the real axis. Simplify powers using i² = −1. When dividing by a non-zero complex number, multiplying by the denominator’s conjugate produces a real denominator.'),
 'Functions, modulus & area estimates': ('Absolute value is a distance', 'Use |u| = u when u ≥ 0 and |u| = −u when u < 0. The expression inside the modulus determines the breakpoint. Solve piecewise and check each candidate against its case condition; a negative right-hand side in |u| = c gives no real solution.'),
 'Waves, sound & superposition': ('Change one wave feature at a time', 'Use amplitude for maximum displacement from equilibrium, wavelength for spacing between matching phases and frequency for cycles per second. At fixed wave speed, increasing frequency shortens wavelength. Superposition adds displacements at each point, so keep sign and phase in the diagram.'),
 'Vectors & motion graphs': ('Translate the representation before calculating', 'A vector needs magnitude and direction. On a position–time graph, gradient gives velocity; on a velocity–time graph, gradient gives acceleration and signed area gives displacement. Area below the time axis is negative displacement. Distance requires adding the magnitudes of the separate displacement areas.'),
 'Moles & stoichiometry': ('Balance first, then use the mole ratio', 'Write correct formulae, balance the equation and convert the known quantity into moles. Use coefficients for the mole ratio, then convert the required amount to mass, volume or particle number using the conditions stated. Coefficients are not automatically mass ratios.'),
 'Photosynthesis & cellular respiration': ('Separate the processes and their roles', 'Photosynthesis stores energy in organic molecules using light; cellular respiration makes energy available for cellular processes. Living plant cells respire as well as photosynthesise. Explain an investigation’s result in terms of the relevant process, controlled variables and a suitable comparison.'),
 'Mitosis & the cell cycle': ('DNA copying and cell division are separate stages', 'DNA replication precedes mitosis. Mitosis distributes copies into daughter nuclei and cytokinesis divides the cell. Keep the distinction between a chromosome, its sister chromatids and the amount of DNA clear when explaining why chromosome number is normally maintained.'),
 'Ecology, nutrient cycles & biodiversity': ('Follow matter and energy separately', 'Read arrows in food relationships as directed transfers, and explain population changes through specific interactions. Nutrients move between organisms and environmental reservoirs. Energy transfers through trophic levels and disperses as heat, so energy flow requires continuing input rather than cycling in the same way as matter.'),
 'Bones, muscles & movement': ('Name the action and the type of contraction', 'In concentric contraction an active muscle shortens; in eccentric contraction it lengthens while producing tension; in isometric contraction tension can be produced without an appreciable change in overall length. Identify the joint action and load before assigning a contraction type.'),
 'Levers & anatomical planes': ('Biomechanics connects position with movement', 'Identify the fulcrum, effort, load and their distances from the pivot before classifying a lever. Torque depends on the perpendicular moment arm. Explain a movement’s plane and perpendicular axis, and connect observations to the task rather than giving an anatomical label without a reason.'),
 'Energy systems & exercise': ('Concurrent systems with changing contributions', 'ATP demand changes with exercise intensity and duration. Phosphagen, glycolytic and aerobic pathways contribute together, with their relative contributions changing during activity and recovery. Avoid describing the systems as isolated switches that take turns being entirely on or off.'),
}

def enrich(topics, subjects, documents):
    for subject in subjects:
        subject['name'] = SUBJECT_NAMES[subject['id']]
    subjects.extend([
      dict(id='english',name='Eng Gr 10J 2026',subtitle='Find your voice. Make your meaning clear.',color='B86027',light='FFF0DF',icon='book'),
      dict(id='re',name='10J RE 2026',subtitle='Reflect, research and put values into practice.',color='4774B3',light='EAF1FE',icon='church'),
    ])
    topics.extend(deepcopy(NEW_TOPICS))
    for topic in topics:
        keywords = topic.pop('keywords', None)
        pattern = SOURCE_PATTERNS.get(topic['title'])
        candidates = [d for d in documents if topic['subject'] in d.get('subjects',[d['subject']])]
        matching = [d for d in candidates if (
            any(k in (d['title']+' '+' '.join(d['sections'])).lower() for k in keywords) if keywords else
            bool(pattern and re.search(pattern, d['title']+' '+' '.join(d['sections']), re.I))
        )]
        topic['classroomSources'] = [d['id'] for d in matching]
        if matching and topic['title'] in CLASSROOM_FOCUS:
            heading, body = CLASSROOM_FOCUS[topic['title']]
            topic['notes'].append(dict(heading=heading,body=body))
            topic['cards'].append(dict(front=heading,back=body))
    return topics, subjects

NEW_TOPICS = []
def pack(subject, title, notes, practice, challenges, cards, keywords, visual='essay', formula='Point → Evidence → Explanation'):
    assert len(notes) >= 3 and len(practice) == 6 and len(challenges) == 2 and len(cards) >= 4
    NEW_TOPICS.append(dict(subject=subject,title=title,group='Classroom',
        id=subject+'-'+re.sub('[^a-z0-9]+','-',title.lower()).strip('-'),
        notes=[dict(heading=h,body=b) for h,b in notes],
        practice=[dict(question=q,answer=a) for q,a in practice],
        challenges=[dict(question=q,answer=a) for q,a in challenges],
        cards=[dict(front=q,back=a) for q,a in notes+practice+challenges+cards],
        sources=[],classroomSources=[],keywords=keywords,visual=visual,formula=formula,
        minutes=8+len(notes)*2,tip='Use the wording and scope of your teacher’s task. Explain an idea in your own words and support it with specific evidence.'))

pack('english','Comprehension & summary',[
 ('Read for purpose, audience and tone','Identify who is speaking, who the text addresses and what it tries to achieve. Distinguish the writer’s attitude (tone) from the language variety or formality (register). Support a tone description with a precise choice of words rather than repeating the label.'),
 ('Build an inference from evidence','Literal questions ask for what the text states; inference questions require a conclusion supported by clues. Use the mark allocation to judge how many distinct ideas to develop. An inference should explain the connection between the evidence and the conclusion, not introduce an unsupported story.'),
 ('A summary is a selection, not a miniature copy','Read the question’s purpose before selecting relevant main ideas. Combine related points, remove examples and repetition where they are not essential, and paraphrase accurately. Follow the requested paragraph or point format and word limit. Do not add your own opinion or change the author’s meaning.')], [
 ('A poster says, “Bring a bottle. Every refill keeps plastic out of our playground.” Identify an audience and purpose.','It addresses learners using the school playground and aims to persuade them to bring reusable bottles. The school setting and direct instruction support this interpretation.'),
 ('A narrator says, “I checked the empty road for the tenth time.” Infer a possible state of mind and support it.','The repeated checking suggests anxiety or impatience while waiting. “For the tenth time” supports the inference; the sentence does not establish exactly what the narrator is waiting for.'),
 ('Distinguish tone from register in a formal letter of complaint.','The register can be formal because of the respectful, conventional language. Its tone may be dissatisfied or firm because of the attitude to the problem. Formality and attitude are different features.'),
 ('Summarise this in one sentence: “The club meets on Mondays. Members share books. They discuss their favourite passages. Reading together introduces them to new ideas.”','The book club meets on Mondays to share books and discuss passages, helping members discover new ideas.'),
 ('A summary task asks for the benefits of walking. Should a detailed story about the writer’s lost shoes be included? Explain.','Only if it supplies a relevant benefit. A vivid anecdote is not automatically a main idea for the task; select by the question’s focus rather than by how memorable a detail is.'),
 ('Explain why swapping a few words for synonyms may still fail as a paraphrase.','The sentence may retain the source’s structure, misrepresent a technical word or leave the main idea unclear. Understand the point first, then express the meaning naturally and accurately.')], [
 ('Two readers infer different motives for a character. Must one inference be wrong? Explain how you would judge them.','Not necessarily. Both may be defensible if the passage supports them. Compare the relevance and strength of each reader’s evidence, consider contradictory clues, and distinguish a plausible inference from unsupported speculation.'),
 ('A summary contains every fact but exceeds the limit. Another meets the limit but changes the writer’s opinion. Explain why neither is a successful summary.','A summary must satisfy both selection/length requirements and accuracy. The first has not prioritised the relevant main ideas; the second misrepresents meaning. Concision and fidelity must work together.')], [
 ('What is a literal answer?','An answer directly supported by information explicitly stated in the text.'),('What makes an inference defensible?','A clear conclusion connected to relevant textual clues.'),('What belongs in a summary?','Main ideas relevant to the stated task, expressed accurately and concisely.'),('Why check the summary’s word count?','The required limit is part of the task and tests selection and concise expression.')],['workbook','scope','outline'])

pack('english','Language & editing',[
 ('Explain a word’s function in its sentence','Parts of speech describe how words function in context. A word such as “light” can be a noun, adjective or verb in different sentences. Identify the actual sentence role rather than memorising one permanent category for every spelling.'),
 ('Punctuation changes relationships','Apostrophes can show possession or omitted letters in contractions. Colons can introduce an explanation, list or quotation; semicolons can connect closely related independent clauses. Explain the particular function in the given sentence, not a list of every possible use.'),
 ('Keep tense, reference and meaning consistent','When changing direct to reported speech, consider pronouns, time expressions and the reporting context. When editing, check subject–verb agreement, tense consistency, sentence boundaries and whether a pronoun has an unambiguous referent. Read the final sentence for meaning after correcting its grammar.')], [
 ('Identify the part of speech of “light” in “They light the candle.”','It is a verb: it expresses the action performed by the subject “they”.'),
 ('Explain the apostrophe in “the learner’s notebook”.','It indicates singular possession: the notebook belongs to one learner. For several learners jointly possessing it, the form would normally be “the learners’ notebook”.'),
 ('Correct “The group of learners are ready” for standard singular agreement with the collective group as one unit.','“The group of learners is ready.” The grammatical head of the subject is the singular “group”; “of learners” is a modifying phrase. Other contexts can treat collective nouns as plural units in varieties of English, so attend to the intended sense.'),
 ('Report this the following day: Anna said, “I am ready today.”','Anna said that she was ready the previous day. Pronoun, tense and time reference change to fit the reporting context.'),
 ('Explain the colon in “She needed three things: paper, a pen and time.”','It introduces the list identifying the three things named in the complete clause before the colon.'),
 ('Rewrite the ambiguous sentence “When Sara met Leila, she was nervous” so it is clear that Leila was nervous.','“Leila was nervous when Sara met her.” Replacing or repositioning the pronoun makes its referent clear.')], [
 ('Why can correctly naming a punctuation mark still fail to answer a question about its effect?','Naming identifies the mark; explaining its effect shows how it structures or changes the meaning in this sentence. An answer must connect the mark to the particular words and relationship it creates.'),
 ('A learner replaces every informal word with a long synonym. Does this guarantee an effective formal register? Explain.','No. Formal register requires appropriate vocabulary, clear syntax, respectful tone and suitability for audience and purpose. An inaccurate or unnecessarily complex synonym can reduce clarity rather than improve the writing.')], [
 ('What is a pronoun referent?','The person or thing a pronoun points to in context.'),('What is a contraction?','A shortened form containing omitted letters, usually marked with an apostrophe, such as “don’t”.'),('What controls subject–verb agreement?','The grammatical subject, interpreted in its sentence context.'),('Why identify parts of speech in context?','The same written word can perform different grammatical functions in different sentences.')],['workbook','scope'])

pack('english','Visual literacy & persuasion',[
 ('Read the visual and verbal messages together','Identify the text’s audience and purpose, then examine layout, size, colour, angle, gesture, facial expression, typography and wording. Link each selected feature to a specific intended effect. Saying that a picture “catches attention” is incomplete without explaining why it attracts this audience and to what message.'),
 ('Explain humour rather than just finding it','A pun activates more than one meaning of a word or phrase. Irony involves a significant contrast between expectation and reality or between apparent and intended meaning. A cartoon can create humour through exaggeration, incongruity, caricature or a mismatch between dialogue and action.'),
 ('Question assumptions and stereotypes','Ask whose viewpoint is made normal, who is excluded and what assumptions the image relies on. Distinguish the message an advertiser intends from whether its claim is supported. A persuasive image can be effective without being a fair or accurate representation.')], [
 ('An advert for a reading club shows a book as a doorway. Explain the metaphor.','The book is represented as an entrance to new worlds or ideas. The visual makes an abstract benefit of reading concrete; connect that invitation to the intended audience.'),
 ('A tired runner stands under the caption “A refreshing start.” Explain a possible irony.','The positive idea of refreshment contrasts with the runner’s visibly exhausted state. The effect depends on the picture and wording being read together.'),
 ('Why might an advert place a large product image above a short slogan?','The size creates a visual hierarchy that directs attention to the product first. The short slogan then frames the message. Explain the actual product and target audience when analysing a real advert.'),
 ('A cartoon’s character says “I love teamwork” while making everyone else do the work. Explain the humour.','There is a mismatch between the character’s statement and behaviour, exposing hypocrisy or selfishness. The criticism arises from the relationship between dialogue and action.'),
 ('What is caricature?','An exaggerated representation of recognisable features used to emphasise a characteristic, often for comic or critical effect.'),
 ('Explain why an attractive product photograph is not sufficient evidence for a health claim.','The photograph can create an impression but does not provide a valid test of the claimed effect. Evaluate the evidence supporting the claim separately from the persuasive presentation.')], [
 ('Two adverts use the same image with different captions. Why can they communicate different messages?','The caption selects and frames possible meanings of the image, directing the viewer toward a particular interpretation. Visual meaning depends on context and its relationship with words, not the image in isolation.'),
 ('A stereotype makes an advert easy to understand. Does that establish that it is harmless or accurate? Explain.','No. Recognisability can make a message efficient while reinforcing a simplified or unfair assumption about a group. Analyse both the persuasive effect and the representation’s assumptions and consequences.')], [
 ('What is visual hierarchy?','The order in which size, position, contrast and other design choices draw attention.'),('What makes a pun work?','A word or phrase supports more than one relevant meaning.'),('What should an effect answer connect?','A specific visual or verbal feature, its meaning and its impact on the audience.'),('What is a stereotype?','An oversimplified generalisation about a group that ignores individual variation.')],['workbook','scope'])

pack('english','Poetry: voice, imagery & argument',[
 ('Move from technique to meaning','Identify the speaker and situation before discussing imagery, sound or structure. A metaphor, repetition or enjambment matters because of what it conveys in the poem. Support an interpretation with a short precise reference, explain its effect and connect it to the poem’s larger concerns.'),
 ('Read the set poems as distinct voices','The Term 1 workbook includes Taufiq Rafat’s “Circumcision”, Chris van Wyk’s “I Have My Father’s Voice”, Stephen Spender’s “My Parents Kept Me from Children Who Were Rough” and John Donne’s “The Flea”. Compare how speakers present childhood, identity, relationships or persuasion without treating their voices as identical to the poets’ own views. The July scope also lists “My Name”; its Term 2 text is not in the posted Term 1 workbook.'),
 ('Test a speaker’s reasoning','In “The Flea”, the speaker develops an elaborate comparison involving a flea to persuade another person. Explaining the conceit is different from agreeing with its reasoning. In Spender’s poem, protection and attraction toward the excluded children create tension: support a reading of that tension with the language actually used. In every poem, distinguish statement, implication and your evaluation.')], [
 ('In an original poem, a speaker calls a silent classroom “a locked cupboard”. Identify and explain the device.','It is a metaphor comparing the room to a cupboard without “like” or “as”. It may suggest confinement or withheld ideas; further interpretation should be checked against the rest of the poem.'),
 ('What is the difference between the poet and the speaker?','The poet is the author; the speaker is the voice constructed in the poem. They may overlap in some ways, but the speaker’s statements cannot automatically be treated as the author’s personal beliefs.'),
 ('Explain the difference between a theme and a summary.','A summary says what happens or is said. A theme states an idea explored through those events or words, such as how protection can also cause isolation.'),
 ('What is a metaphysical conceit, using “The Flea” as an example without quoting it?','It is an extended, intellectually elaborate comparison between apparently unlike things. Donne’s speaker uses the flea and its shared blood to construct an argument about the relationship between the speaker and the person addressed.'),
 ('Why is “the poet uses alliteration for effect” insufficient analysis?','It names a technique but does not identify the repeated sound, explain its local impact or connect it to the poem’s meaning.'),
 ('How would you approach an unseen poem before identifying figures of speech?','Read the whole poem, identify the speaker, situation, movement of ideas and possible tone shifts. Then use specific language and structure to develop and test an interpretation.')], [
 ('A speaker makes an emotionally powerful but logically weak argument. Can a poem still be effective? Explain.', 'Yes. The poem may reveal the speaker’s desire, manipulation or self-deception through that weakness. Analyse what the argument exposes about the voice and relationship rather than assuming the poem endorses every claim it contains.'),
 ('Two readers describe the same poem’s ending as hopeful and uncertain. How can you evaluate both readings fairly?', 'Find precise evidence for hope and for unresolved doubt, compare the ending with earlier images or tone, and judge whether a combined or qualified reading explains more of the text. Do not decide only by which label sounds more positive.')], [
 ('What is a lyric speaker?','The voice or perspective constructed in a poem.'),('What is enjambment?','A sentence or phrase continuing beyond a line break without a full syntactic stop.'),('What is tone?','The attitude conveyed by the voice and language toward a subject or audience.'),('What should follow naming a device?','A precise reference and an explanation of its effect on meaning in context.')],['workbook','poetry','scope'])

pack('english','A Thousand Splendid Suns: character & theme',[
 ('Track changing relationships','Khaled Hosseini’s novel follows Mariam and Laila, women from different generations whose lives become connected amid conflict and loss in Afghanistan. Build a character chart recording a character’s circumstances, choices, relationships and changes. Keep track of the narrator’s perspective and avoid using knowledge of later chapters as evidence for an earlier contextual extract.'),
 ('Turn a theme into an argument','A topic such as family, oppression, love or resilience becomes an analytical argument when you explain what the novel suggests about it. Select an episode, identify an action or narrative detail, explain its significance and connect it to your claim. A character’s suffering is not by itself a complete explanation of a theme.'),
 ('Answer within your reading scope','The July scope says to study characters and themes up to the point reached in class. Use your own edition and class reading boundary when locating evidence. Prepare brief episode references and accurate short quotations from your copy; do not invent quotations or assume these generated study notes replace reading the novel.')], [
 ('What information belongs in a character-development chart?','Initial circumstances, important relationships, significant choices, relevant textual evidence and how the character changes over time.'),
 ('Improve the topic label “friendship” into a possible analytical claim to test against your reading.','For example: “The developing friendship can offer support when external circumstances limit personal freedom.” This is a claim to support or qualify with episodes, not a fact established just by naming the theme.'),
 ('Why should a contextual answer begin with the extract rather than a whole-novel plot summary?','The question tests interpretation of particular language, actions and circumstances. Brief context can clarify the extract, but a broad summary may never explain the detail being asked about.'),
 ('How can you compare two characters without writing two unrelated biographies?','Choose one shared question, such as their responses to a conflict, and compare their choices and evidence directly. Explain both similarities and differences under the same criterion.'),
 ('A paragraph says “This shows resilience” after an episode. What reasoning is missing?','Explain what obstacle the character faces, what response demonstrates resilience, and how that detail supports the paragraph’s claim. Merely attaching a theme label does not establish the connection.'),
 ('What should you do if you cannot remember a quotation exactly?','Use an accurate episode reference or paraphrase where the task allows it, and verify the quotation in your edition during revision. Do not put invented wording in quotation marks.')], [
 ('Can a character be constrained by social circumstances and still make meaningful choices? Explain how to build a balanced argument.','Yes. Identify the actual limits on available options, then examine the choices the character makes within those limits. Support both constraint and agency with specific episodes rather than treating freedom as simply present or absent.'),
 ('A reader admires a character and therefore calls every decision wise. What is the flaw in that method?', 'Admiration does not establish the consequences or quality of each decision. Evaluate motives, available information, alternatives and outcomes in context. A sympathetic character can still make mistakes, and recognising complexity can strengthen an interpretation.')], [
 ('Who wrote A Thousand Splendid Suns?','Khaled Hosseini.'),('What is a contextual question?','A question about a particular extract, understood in its relevant narrative situation.'),('What makes a thematic claim useful?','It states an interpretable idea about a topic and can be tested against specific textual evidence.'),('Why verify chapter scope?','The teacher’s task may assess only the part of the novel reached in class.')],['workbook','scope'],visual='narrative')

pack('english','Macbeth: ambition, choices & consequences',[
 ('Follow the causal chain','Shakespeare’s tragedy follows Macbeth’s response to predictions of kingship. He and Lady Macbeth plan Duncan’s murder; gaining power leads to further fear and violence. When revising, separate what a character hears, what they decide and what follows. Prophecy is not the same as an instruction that removes personal responsibility.'),
 ('Analyse dramatic methods','A soliloquy lets the audience hear a character thinking aloud; dramatic irony arises when the audience knows something a character does not. Repeated images, contrasting scenes and ambiguous language develop meaning. Explain what the audience understands at the precise point in the play, not only what happens later.'),
 ('Build a literature essay','Start with a clear answer to the essay question and plan paragraphs that develop different parts of that answer. Each paragraph needs a relevant claim, accurate evidence, analysis of language or action, and a link to the argument. Address complexity or a counterpoint. The July scope includes plot, characters, themes, symbols and quotations; check act and scene references in your edition.')], [
 ('Why is “Macbeth is ambitious” a starting point rather than a complete essay argument?','It identifies a trait but does not explain how ambition shapes choices, interacts with other pressures or produces consequences. Develop a claim that answers the actual question and can be supported across scenes.'),
 ('What can a soliloquy reveal that dialogue with another character may conceal?','Private doubts, conflicting motives or plans that the character may hide from others. The contrast between public speech and private thought can be analytically important.'),
 ('Distinguish a prediction from a command in analysing the witches’ influence.','A prediction states an anticipated outcome; a command directs an action. Examine how Macbeth interprets the prediction and chooses to act rather than assuming it explicitly orders every later deed.'),
 ('What should follow a quotation in a literature essay?','Explain the significant word, image, contrast or action and show how it supports the paragraph’s claim. Quotation without analysis does not establish an argument.'),
 ('Why should recurring blood imagery be discussed in context rather than given one fixed meaning everywhere?','Its associations can develop with the scene, speaker and action. Test whether it evokes violence, guilt or another concern at that point instead of mechanically applying one label.'),
 ('How does a conclusion differ from a final plot summary?','A conclusion draws together the argument and gives a reasoned answer to the question. It should synthesise what the analysis has established rather than retell the ending.')], [
 ('If a character’s action was predicted, does that prove the character had no responsibility for it? Explain the distinction an essay should make.','A prediction alone does not establish coercion or the absence of alternatives. Analyse the character’s knowledge, deliberation, pressures and choices. A strong essay can discuss fate and agency together while grounding its conclusion in scenes.'),
 ('Why can an essay that acknowledges an alternative interpretation be stronger than one that ignores it?','Addressing a plausible alternative shows that the argument has been tested against more than one reading. Explain why the selected evidence supports your conclusion or qualifies it; simply listing opposing views without evaluating them is not enough.')], [
 ('What is a soliloquy?','A speech that presents a character’s thoughts aloud, conventionally unheard by other characters.'),('What is dramatic irony?','A difference between what the audience knows and what a character knows.'),('What is an essay thesis?','The central arguable answer to the essay question.'),('What should a quotation do?','Provide precise evidence for an argument that is explained through analysis.')],['scope'],visual='narrative')

pack('english','Transactional writing: purpose & register',[
 ('Plan for a real audience','Identify the writer’s role, recipient, purpose, format and required content before drafting. A diary entry can explore a private immediate response; a letter of application presents a relevant case to an organisation. Choose details and register to fit that relationship rather than using the same voice for every form.'),
 ('Make structure serve the purpose','The July scope lists a friendly letter, letter of application, magazine article, diary entry and speech. Revise the conventions in the teacher’s handbook or examples, including appropriate openings and closings. Organise paragraphs so each advances the task; do not let a memorised template displace the actual content requested.'),
 ('Draft, then edit for effect','Plan a few distinct ideas and develop them coherently. Check whether the opening establishes purpose, the middle provides relevant detail and the ending achieves a suitable final impression. Edit agreement, punctuation, tense, repetition and word count. Formal writing can be clear and direct; complicated wording is not a requirement for quality.')], [
 ('What changes when the same event is described in a diary and in a formal complaint?','Audience and purpose change. A diary may explore private feelings, while a complaint should identify the problem, provide relevant facts and request a reasonable response in an appropriate register.'),
 ('Write a concise original opening for an application to join a school reading committee.','“I would like to apply to join the school reading committee. My experience organising our class book exchange would help me contribute to its reading events.” The opening states purpose and a relevant reason.'),
 ('Why should a magazine article’s heading relate closely to its content?','It sets expectations and invites the intended audience to read. A striking heading that promises a different subject may undermine coherence and credibility.'),
 ('What is the risk of using slang in a letter of application?','It may be inappropriate for the relationship with the recipient and weaken the impression of professionalism. The issue is suitability for audience and purpose, not that informal language is always wrong.'),
 ('How can a speech conclusion connect to its introduction?','It can return to an opening image, question or idea and show how the speech’s argument has developed it. The link should reinforce the final message rather than repeat the whole speech.'),
 ('Why is a correctly formatted letter not automatically a successful response?','The content may fail to answer the task, suit the audience or develop a clear purpose. Format is one part of an effective response, alongside relevant ideas, organisation and language.')], [
 ('Can two different registers both be appropriate for the same writing form? Explain with a letter.','Yes. A friendly letter to a close relative and a letter to an unfamiliar organisation use the same broad medium but different relationships and purposes. Format alone does not determine every language choice.'),
 ('An application lists many achievements but never relates them to the role. Why might a shorter application be more persuasive?','Relevant evidence matters more than the number of claims. A shorter response that explains how selected experience meets the role’s needs creates a clearer case for the recipient.')], [
 ('What five things should you identify before writing?','Role, audience, purpose, format and required content.'),('What is register?','Language choices suited to the situation, relationship and level of formality.'),('What is coherence?','The logical connection of ideas into a meaningful whole.'),('What should editing check beyond spelling?','Meaning, structure, agreement, tense, punctuation, repetition, register and task requirements.')],['scope','workbook'])

pack('english','Prepared speaking, reading & recital',[
 ('Write for listening','A prepared speech needs a clear introduction, a well-organised body and a purposeful conclusion. Make transitions audible, explain unfamiliar ideas and use relevant evidence. The workbook recommends preparation and research; a dramatic performance is not a substitute for a coherent formal speech.'),
 ('Make delivery support meaning','Practise pronunciation, pace, volume, pauses, eye contact and natural gesture. In prepared reading, phrasing should clarify sentence structure and tone. In poetry recital, stress and pauses should reveal the poem’s meaning rather than impose an identical sing-song rhythm on every line.'),
 ('Use the actual rubric','Compare a rehearsal with the posted reading or recital rubric. Distinguish content preparation from delivery, and select one improvement for the next rehearsal. Know the piece well enough to communicate with the audience; expressive delivery should serve meaning, not distract from it.')], [
 ('Why can speaking more slowly at an important point help an audience?','It gives listeners time to process a key idea and can signal emphasis. Vary pace deliberately; making the entire speech unnaturally slow may weaken its flow.'),
 ('How can a speaker establish purpose without simply announcing “My speech is about…”?','Open with a relevant question, brief example or striking idea, then connect it clearly to the topic and direction of the speech. The device should serve the purpose rather than be an unrelated performance.'),
 ('Why should a reader pause at a change in meaning rather than at every printed line ending?','Sentence structure and punctuation may continue across a line break. Phrasing should make the meaning clear; an automatic pause at each line can distort a sentence or poem.'),
 ('What is one useful way to rehearse with a rubric?','Choose a criterion such as audibility or organisation, practise the whole section, obtain specific feedback against that criterion, and rehearse again with one planned change.'),
 ('Why can looking up from notes improve a prepared speech?','It helps maintain audience contact and allows the speaker to notice whether listeners are following. Preparation should make these moments possible without losing the thread of the argument.'),
 ('Explain why louder delivery is not always more expressive delivery.','Expression also depends on pace, stress, tone and pause. A consistently loud voice can flatten differences in meaning; use volume suitable for the audience and room.')], [
 ('A recital is fluent but its dramatic emphasis contradicts the words. Has fluent memorisation alone met the purpose? Explain.','No. Fluency supports communication, but the delivery must demonstrate understanding of the text. Stress and pauses should be justified by the poem’s voice, situation and movement of ideas.'),
 ('Two speakers use identical words. Why might one be easier to understand?', 'Phrasing, pace, articulation, emphasis and audience contact influence how listeners process the message. Delivery can clarify relationships between ideas even when the written text is the same.')], [
 ('What is phrasing in oral reading?','Grouping words and using pauses to make grammar and meaning clear.'),('What should gesture support?','The meaning and purpose of the speech rather than unrelated movement.'),('What makes rehearsal feedback useful?','It identifies a specific observable feature and a practical change.'),('Why learn a poem’s meaning before reciting it?','Interpretation guides appropriate stress, pace, tone and pauses.')],['reading rubric','poetry','workbook','recital'])

pack('re','Eucharist, miracles & evaluating sources',[
 ('State the Catholic teaching accurately','In Catholic teaching the Eucharist involves Christ’s real presence. Transubstantiation describes a change of substance while the appearances of bread and wine remain. A report of an extraordinary visible event is therefore different from the sacramental teaching itself. Present a belief as Catholic teaching, and a reported event as a claim to investigate.'),
 ('Keep an observation separate from its interpretation','For the Classroom research task, distinguish the account of an event, the people or institutions investigating it, the reported findings and the interpretation attached to them. A statement on a popular webpage is not automatically the original scientific report. Record exactly what each source supports and what it does not establish.'),
 ('Build a traceable research record','For one chosen event, record the place, date, source author, publication date, investigation details and relevant limitations. Compare sources, cite the original report where available, and explain religious relevance in your own reasoned words. Do not invent a named investigator, tissue finding or Church judgement when your sources do not verify it.')], [
 ('Distinguish the Eucharist’s Catholic sacramental teaching from a reported Eucharistic miracle.','The sacramental teaching concerns Christ’s real presence and transubstantiation. A reported miracle describes an extraordinary claimed event requiring its own historical and evidential examination. The two are not interchangeable.'),
 ('A website says “scientists proved it” but names no researchers or report. What information should a research note seek?','Named investigators or institutions, the original report, dates, methods, the material examined, results and limitations. The phrase alone does not allow readers to trace or evaluate the evidence.'),
 ('What belongs in a bibliography entry for a webpage?','At minimum, the available author or responsible organisation, page title, publication/update date where stated, URL and access date, using the teacher’s required citation style.'),
 ('Why should you distinguish the date of an event from the date of its investigation?','They describe different stages in the evidence chain. A later investigation does not automatically supply direct evidence for every detail reported about an earlier event.'),
 ('Give a respectful way to distinguish belief from a test result in writing.','For example: “Catholics may interpret the reported event in relation to Eucharistic belief; the report’s stated observation is …”. Complete the observation only with a finding your source actually documents.'),
 ('Why should the religious-relevance section do more than repeat the event’s description?','It asks for an explained connection to Catholic belief or practice and a motivated evaluation. Retelling the event does not by itself show its relevance or support a personal conclusion.')], [
 ('A report establishes that a sample contains human tissue. Does that observation alone establish every historical and theological claim made about the sample? Explain.','No. Identify exactly what the test establishes, how the sample was obtained and authenticated, and what other claims require separate evidence. A measured observation, a historical account and a theological interpretation are different kinds of claims.'),
 ('Two webpages repeat the same story word for word. Why is this not necessarily independent corroboration?', 'They may both copy one earlier account. Trace their sources and assess the original evidence rather than counting repeated webpages as separate investigations. Independent corroboration requires evidence not merely repeated wording.')], [
 ('What does transubstantiation describe in Catholic teaching?','A change of the substance of bread and wine while their appearances remain.'),('What is a primary research source?','An original report or evidence directly relevant to the claim, rather than a later summary alone.'),('Why record a source’s limitations?','They define what its evidence can reasonably support.'),('What should you do when a claimed scientific finding cannot be verified?','Identify it as unverified and avoid supplying invented details.')],['eucharistic'],visual='research',formula='Claim → Evidence → Interpretation')

pack('re','Saints, virtue & reflective research',[
 ('Connect a virtue with an action','The Saint Project asks for a biography and an explanation of admired qualities. A virtue is more than a flattering label: explain the concrete action, the difficulty involved and the quality the action illustrates. Catholic recognition of a saint concerns a life of holiness; distinguish the historical evidence about a person from your interpretation of their example.'),
 ('Organise biography as a meaningful sequence','Record dates and places, family circumstances, occupation, significant events and the circumstances of death using reliable sources. Select details that help explain the person’s choices. Verify a quotation’s wording and attribution before using it; an attractive quotation found online is not proof that the person said it.'),
 ('Make reflection practical and specific','The teacher’s brief also asks for qualities you aspire to and concrete ways to develop them. Connect an admired action with a realistic action in your own context. Explain why you selected the person, give credit for images and information, and prepare to answer questions rather than simply reading a list of facts.')], [
 ('Turn “the saint was generous” into a research question that could support an explanation.','Ask what the person gave or did, for whom, at what cost or difficulty, and which sources document it. Those details can support a reasoned account of generosity.'),
 ('Why is a date-and-place timeline useful before drafting a biography?','It checks sequence, helps identify contradictions and places significant choices in context. It supports a coherent account rather than an unordered list of facts.'),
 ('Give a concrete application of the quality “service” in a school context.','For example, commit to a specific regular task that meets a real need, such as helping maintain a class resource collection, and reflect on whether it helps others. The example should be practical rather than a vague promise to be better.'),
 ('A quotation appears on several unattributed image cards. What should you check before presenting it?','Find a reliable attribution to the person or an original work, verify the wording and context, and cite it. If it cannot be verified, do not present it as an authenticated quotation.'),
 ('Why should a presentation include reasons for choosing the saint?','The task includes personal reflection: explain which documented qualities matter to you and how the example connects to your own aims, rather than relying only on fame or an attractive image.'),
 ('How can an image support a presentation rather than decorate it?','Use an image that helps explain the person, setting or point being discussed, identify what it shows and credit its source. Visuals should contribute to understanding.')], [
 ('Can a biography be factually detailed yet weak at explaining virtue? Explain the missing link.', 'Yes. Dates and events establish information, but an explanation of virtue must connect particular choices and circumstances to a quality and justify that interpretation. The task requires both knowledge and reflection.'),
 ('Two sources disagree about a saint’s early life. Why should a researcher report the uncertainty instead of combining both accounts into one confident story?', 'Combining incompatible claims can manufacture an account that no source supports. Compare dates, provenance and evidence, explain the disagreement and qualify the conclusion where verification is not possible.')], [
 ('What turns a virtue label into an explanation?','A documented action, its circumstances and a reasoned connection to the quality.'),('What does a bibliography let the reader do?','Trace the sources of information, quotations and images.'),('What makes a personal application concrete?','A specific feasible action connected to the admired quality.'),('Why practise answering questions?','It demonstrates understanding beyond memorising the presentation.')],['saint'],visual='research',formula='Life → Choices → Virtues → Application')
